So the office hour
this week is moving to?

00:00:11.700 --> 00:00:12.270
Thursday.

00:00:12.270 --> 00:00:13.410
Thursday?

00:00:13.410 --> 00:00:14.910
Thursday same time.

00:00:14.910 --> 00:00:16.440
Yeah, to go vote.

00:00:16.440 --> 00:00:20.100
it's so that you can all
go vote, so he can go vote.

00:00:20.100 --> 00:00:22.560
I don't even know
exactly for what.

00:00:22.560 --> 00:00:28.890
I haven't being keeping track,
but go vote for something good.

00:00:28.890 --> 00:00:34.410
And what we're going to do
today is continue the discussion

00:00:34.410 --> 00:00:42.620
about lossy compression,
which Pulkit started.

00:00:42.620 --> 00:00:44.430
[INAUDIBLE]

00:00:44.430 --> 00:00:47.340
OK, thanks for the reminder.

00:00:47.340 --> 00:00:50.310
Constantly remind until
we get to the right size.

00:00:50.310 --> 00:00:58.320
Lossy compression that
Pulkit began to expose--

00:00:58.320 --> 00:01:03.840
we're going to further develop
this notion and the setting

00:01:03.840 --> 00:01:08.650
around lossy compression
and get to also

00:01:08.650 --> 00:01:16.900
the notion of lossy compression
and rate-distortion theory

00:01:16.900 --> 00:01:20.620
or-- and the rate-distortion
function, which

00:01:20.620 --> 00:01:28.210
is the analog and the extension
of the notion of entropy

00:01:28.210 --> 00:01:34.780
from lossless compression,
which you emerged as the answer

00:01:34.780 --> 00:01:38.920
to what is the fundamental
limit on lossless compression.

00:01:38.920 --> 00:01:43.480
We're going to see that the
rate-distortion function is what

00:01:43.480 --> 00:01:47.260
emerges in characterizing
the fundamental limits

00:01:47.260 --> 00:01:50.830
on lossy compression.

00:01:50.830 --> 00:01:56.710
And for that, we're going
to start with reminders

00:01:56.710 --> 00:02:01.960
of some of the most
basic quantities

00:02:01.960 --> 00:02:05.950
that we've already been
exposed to extensively

00:02:05.950 --> 00:02:09.400
in the context of lossless
compression and first,

00:02:09.400 --> 00:02:12.850
and foremost was the
notion of entropy.

00:02:12.850 --> 00:02:20.110
So if we had, say, a random
variable, a random object, X,

00:02:20.110 --> 00:02:26.140
which was discrete,
so X is, let's say,

00:02:26.140 --> 00:02:30.310
a discrete random variable--

00:02:30.310 --> 00:02:36.970
OK, random variable or
random object that takes

00:02:36.970 --> 00:02:44.170
values in some discrete,
finite, maybe alphabet.

00:02:44.170 --> 00:02:48.070
Then, it made sense to
talk about its entropy.

00:02:48.070 --> 00:02:55.600
So then we can talk about the
entropy of X. And that was--

00:02:55.600 --> 00:03:03.970
so we denoted it
by h of x, and that

00:03:03.970 --> 00:03:08.650
was defined to be
right the sum over all

00:03:08.650 --> 00:03:14.710
the possible values, small X,
that this random object can

00:03:14.710 --> 00:03:15.670
take.

00:03:15.670 --> 00:03:24.100
The probability that it
takes that value times log,

00:03:24.100 --> 00:03:30.100
by default, base two, log base
two of 1 over the probability

00:03:30.100 --> 00:03:34.240
that it assumes, that value
summed up over all the values.

00:03:34.240 --> 00:03:39.400
And we saw how
fundamental this quantity

00:03:39.400 --> 00:03:42.070
is in the context of
compression, lossless

00:03:42.070 --> 00:03:43.000
compression.

00:03:43.000 --> 00:03:46.120
It answers the question
right even more specifically,

00:03:46.120 --> 00:03:51.670
if your data were to
come from or be generated

00:03:51.670 --> 00:03:58.930
by this a source, if it if it
were a memoryless, like if it

00:03:58.930 --> 00:04:05.590
were IID according to the
distribution of this generic X,

00:04:05.590 --> 00:04:12.040
then the best that you can hope
to do in compressing data coming

00:04:12.040 --> 00:04:14.740
from such a source,
even if you do--

00:04:14.740 --> 00:04:23.320
if you try to be as resourceful
as you can wish for and work

00:04:23.320 --> 00:04:28.780
in blocks, large blocks, the
best you can hope to do is--

00:04:28.780 --> 00:04:32.680
in terms of compression,
is these many bits

00:04:32.680 --> 00:04:40.090
per source symbol, per component
of your data which is generated,

00:04:40.090 --> 00:04:43.660
IID X.

00:04:43.660 --> 00:04:50.500
And now, of course, this carries
over you trivially almost to,

00:04:50.500 --> 00:04:52.270
let's say, if you have a pair.

00:04:52.270 --> 00:05:03.830
So now X and Y are a pair of
discrete random variables.

00:05:09.420 --> 00:05:12.450
Then, we can talk about
their joint entropy.

00:05:12.450 --> 00:05:20.610
So entropy of the
pair X,Y, and we can--

00:05:20.610 --> 00:05:29.640
and we denote that by H of
this pair, the H of X,Y.

00:05:29.640 --> 00:05:34.350
And that is really nothing new.

00:05:34.350 --> 00:05:37.800
It's nothing but this
entropy, this time associated

00:05:37.800 --> 00:05:40.050
with what random object--

00:05:40.050 --> 00:05:44.610
this time, the random object
is this pair, just like x,

00:05:44.610 --> 00:05:46.380
was a random object
and took values

00:05:46.380 --> 00:05:49.050
in some space of
possible values.

00:05:49.050 --> 00:05:52.770
Well, now this pair of
variables is also jointly

00:05:52.770 --> 00:05:59.220
a random object taking values
in some alphabet of pairs.

00:05:59.220 --> 00:06:01.980
And so it's really
exactly this thing.

00:06:01.980 --> 00:06:05.850
It's the entropy associated
with the random object,

00:06:05.850 --> 00:06:07.710
which is the pair, X,Y.

00:06:07.710 --> 00:06:15.430
So it's going to be the sum over
all the values small x small y

00:06:15.430 --> 00:06:22.480
that this pair of random objects
can take, the probability that X

00:06:22.480 --> 00:06:29.140
is equal to small x and that
Y is equal to small y log of 1

00:06:29.140 --> 00:06:31.820
over this probability.

00:06:37.750 --> 00:06:42.130
OK, so obviously, in terms
of operational significance,

00:06:42.130 --> 00:06:45.260
it carries over from the
operational significance

00:06:45.260 --> 00:06:45.760
of this.

00:06:45.760 --> 00:06:52.210
If your data happens to
comprise IID pairs coming

00:06:52.210 --> 00:06:55.930
from the distribution
of this pair,

00:06:55.930 --> 00:06:58.990
then the best that you can
do for compressing this data

00:06:58.990 --> 00:07:07.180
is this, in terms of
number of bits per pair.

00:07:07.180 --> 00:07:10.450
A slightly, maybe
less trivial extension

00:07:10.450 --> 00:07:13.360
of the notion of entropy
that we've already seen

00:07:13.360 --> 00:07:17.250
is conditional entropy.

00:07:17.250 --> 00:07:30.970
So let's say the conditional,
so conditional entropy of,

00:07:30.970 --> 00:07:36.850
I don't know, let's say of X,
of one of these random objects,

00:07:36.850 --> 00:07:47.180
say X given the other, given
Y. So that, too, we defined

00:07:47.180 --> 00:07:47.870
and we used.

00:07:47.870 --> 00:07:53.030
And [? Shubham ?] used it also
in the context of modeling

00:07:53.030 --> 00:07:56.990
sources that go beyond IID.

00:07:56.990 --> 00:08:03.350
So there, the conditional
entropy of X given Y

00:08:03.350 --> 00:08:08.930
is, well, probably
the most natural way

00:08:08.930 --> 00:08:16.100
of presenting it is as a sum
over all the possible values

00:08:16.100 --> 00:08:22.880
that this Y variable can take,
so a sum over all the small ys

00:08:22.880 --> 00:08:25.700
that Y can take.

00:08:25.700 --> 00:08:32.250
And then we're going
to wait-- we're

00:08:32.250 --> 00:08:37.919
going to take the probability
that this random variable

00:08:37.919 --> 00:08:41.880
or object assumes
the value small y.

00:08:41.880 --> 00:08:44.520
That's going to be the
weight that we assigned

00:08:44.520 --> 00:08:51.510
to the conditional
entropy of X, given

00:08:51.510 --> 00:08:54.780
that Y is equal to small y.

00:08:58.510 --> 00:09:06.880
And what is this conditional
entropy that X is equal to--

00:09:06.880 --> 00:09:09.490
of X, given that Y
is equal to small y?

00:09:09.490 --> 00:09:16.030
Well, that's really back
to this notion of entropy.

00:09:16.030 --> 00:09:20.860
OK, this time associated--
so entropy, in the end,

00:09:20.860 --> 00:09:25.600
even though we denote it
H of a random variable.

00:09:25.600 --> 00:09:28.960
It's really a function
of the distribution

00:09:28.960 --> 00:09:31.930
of that random variable.

00:09:31.930 --> 00:09:38.710
So here, this entropy is a
function of the probability mass

00:09:38.710 --> 00:09:44.500
function that describes X, well,
here, this creature is exactly

00:09:44.500 --> 00:09:46.510
this creature.

00:09:46.510 --> 00:09:49.840
The role of the probability
mass function now

00:09:49.840 --> 00:09:54.910
is played by the conditional
probability mass function of X

00:09:54.910 --> 00:10:01.900
when we know that Y takes on
this particular value small y.

00:10:01.900 --> 00:10:11.590
OK, so this thing is
precisely this thing.

00:10:11.590 --> 00:10:15.670
So I'm going to sum up
over all the values, small

00:10:15.670 --> 00:10:17.380
x that X can take.

00:10:17.380 --> 00:10:21.190
This time, it's going to be the
role of the probability mass

00:10:21.190 --> 00:10:25.240
function on x will be played by
the conditional probability mass

00:10:25.240 --> 00:10:31.390
function on x, given that
y is equal to some value,

00:10:31.390 --> 00:10:34.330
this particular value
we're conditioning on.

00:10:45.830 --> 00:10:53.240
OK, so again, I'm assuming this
is familiar and, in fact, one

00:10:53.240 --> 00:10:54.710
thing that you've seen.

00:10:54.710 --> 00:10:57.620
Oh, OK, so this we gave it.

00:10:57.620 --> 00:11:02.810
This has the
notation h of x given

00:11:02.810 --> 00:11:11.300
y, H of the random variable x
given the random variable y.

00:11:11.300 --> 00:11:15.260
Again, it's a function really
of their joint probability mass

00:11:15.260 --> 00:11:17.810
function.

00:11:17.810 --> 00:11:21.320
And here it is explicitly this.

00:11:21.320 --> 00:11:24.680
Again, in the end,
this is a number.

00:11:24.680 --> 00:11:28.010
Again, I know sometimes, for
some of you, the notation,

00:11:28.010 --> 00:11:30.740
and I feel for you--
for some of you

00:11:30.740 --> 00:11:34.160
this is sometimes confusing
because, wait, H of X,

00:11:34.160 --> 00:11:38.030
it's a function of X, so
it's not a random variable.

00:11:38.030 --> 00:11:41.660
It's a number that depends
on the distribution of X.

00:11:41.660 --> 00:11:43.940
And so here, too,
write this thing is

00:11:43.940 --> 00:11:49.610
a number, as are all of these.

00:11:49.610 --> 00:11:53.060
For any small value y,
these are different numbers.

00:11:53.060 --> 00:11:58.280
And as and one of the
things that you'll recall

00:11:58.280 --> 00:12:02.690
is the property of this
conditional entropy

00:12:02.690 --> 00:12:07.280
is that conditioning
reduces entropy.

00:12:07.280 --> 00:12:12.350
So the conditional
entropy of X, given Y,

00:12:12.350 --> 00:12:21.098
is always no more
than the entropy of X.

00:12:21.098 --> 00:12:32.840
So recall, and that's
also pretty intuitive.

00:12:32.840 --> 00:12:36.500
If we think also of entropy
as a notion of uncertainty,

00:12:36.500 --> 00:12:38.990
then you're--

00:12:38.990 --> 00:12:41.090
on average, your
uncertainty on x

00:12:41.090 --> 00:12:45.410
given that something
like Y information

00:12:45.410 --> 00:12:48.020
can only help to
reduce the uncertainty.

00:12:48.020 --> 00:12:56.180
So you're going to always have
this relationship, even though,

00:12:56.180 --> 00:13:00.200
in this also something
that Shubham pointed out.

00:13:00.200 --> 00:13:03.200
It's possible that for
some specific values of Y,

00:13:03.200 --> 00:13:09.410
this thing is suddenly
larger than the entropy of X.

00:13:09.410 --> 00:13:11.480
Suddenly, maybe
there's some value

00:13:11.480 --> 00:13:14.420
of Y, which is kind of
atypical and throws you off,

00:13:14.420 --> 00:13:17.180
and suddenly you see, wait,
Y assumed that value, all

00:13:17.180 --> 00:13:22.020
of a sudden, you're totally
clueless about what X might be.

00:13:22.020 --> 00:13:23.190
So maybe.

00:13:23.190 --> 00:13:30.810
But on average, when you average
these over all of the possible,

00:13:30.810 --> 00:13:31.980
when you--

00:13:31.980 --> 00:13:33.840
over all the
possible values of Y,

00:13:33.840 --> 00:13:36.150
and you give them the
appropriate weight,

00:13:36.150 --> 00:13:40.380
then you're always going to
have on average reduction

00:13:40.380 --> 00:13:44.700
of uncertainty with knowledge
of something, in this case,

00:13:44.700 --> 00:13:49.800
knowledge of Y can only help
reduce your uncertainty about X.

00:13:49.800 --> 00:13:53.580
And also, operationally, in
the context of compression,

00:13:53.580 --> 00:13:59.130
this was also kind of implicit,
at least if not explicit,

00:13:59.130 --> 00:14:03.870
in what Shubham talked about
in the context of compression.

00:14:03.870 --> 00:14:09.390
This corresponds
to, for example,

00:14:09.390 --> 00:14:12.810
the fundamental limit on
how well you can compress,

00:14:12.810 --> 00:14:14.970
let's say, data coming.

00:14:14.970 --> 00:14:18.120
Let's say if your
data is coming--

00:14:18.120 --> 00:14:22.940
let's say you have
pairs coming IID from

00:14:22.940 --> 00:14:26.720
according-- to
this pair, XI, YIs,

00:14:26.720 --> 00:14:29.810
and then you have
the sequence of Ys

00:14:29.810 --> 00:14:32.000
as side information
for your compression.

00:14:32.000 --> 00:14:35.540
So both encoder and decoder
know the y sequence.

00:14:35.540 --> 00:14:37.190
Now, we want to
understand what's

00:14:37.190 --> 00:14:40.700
the best we can do for
compressing X. Then,

00:14:40.700 --> 00:14:44.300
the best that we
can do is in terms

00:14:44.300 --> 00:14:46.910
of bits per source
component is going

00:14:46.910 --> 00:14:50.690
to be this conditional entropy.

00:14:50.690 --> 00:14:58.070
OK, so obviously, information
can only help you to do better.

00:14:58.070 --> 00:14:59.390
You can always ignore it.

00:14:59.390 --> 00:15:04.010
So we're always going to
have this relationship.

00:15:04.010 --> 00:15:09.710
OK, again, I'm assuming that
this is kind of familiar, and--

00:15:09.710 --> 00:15:13.265
but of course, feel free
to stop me at any point.

00:15:16.970 --> 00:15:24.410
And this is enough to
present the possibly the most

00:15:24.410 --> 00:15:28.970
important new quantity
in today's lecture, which

00:15:28.970 --> 00:15:32.930
is the mutual information
between X and Y.

00:15:32.930 --> 00:15:38.315
So maybe I'll make sense
if I continue here.

00:15:43.300 --> 00:15:45.410
Can I continue here?

00:15:45.410 --> 00:15:53.540
OK, this is going to be attached
to those who are following me

00:15:53.540 --> 00:15:54.500
so you know the order.

00:15:54.500 --> 00:15:59.720
So now we can talk about--
in the context of X and Y,

00:15:59.720 --> 00:16:06.170
we can talk about the
mutual information.

00:16:10.960 --> 00:16:25.420
OK, between X and Y, and that
we denote for historical reasons

00:16:25.420 --> 00:16:30.940
by I. Well, OK, that makes
sense, I, information.

00:16:30.940 --> 00:16:35.110
And we denote it
as I, and then we--

00:16:35.110 --> 00:16:40.000
instead of X, comma Y,
for historical reasons

00:16:40.000 --> 00:16:43.060
that I myself don't
fully understand.

00:16:43.060 --> 00:16:46.030
We're going to
write a semicolon.

00:16:46.030 --> 00:16:56.370
So I (X; Y) is our notation for
the mutual information between X

00:16:56.370 --> 00:17:01.830
and Y, which is defined
as, or let's say,

00:17:01.830 --> 00:17:04.260
in the context of
our current context

00:17:04.260 --> 00:17:06.450
of discrete random variables.

00:17:06.450 --> 00:17:11.160
It can be defined as
the entropy of one

00:17:11.160 --> 00:17:16.290
of the variables plus
the entropy of the other

00:17:16.290 --> 00:17:18.570
minus their joint entropy.

00:17:24.520 --> 00:17:37.060
OK, so that's what mutual
information between X and Y is.

00:17:37.060 --> 00:17:39.070
That's how it's defined.

00:17:39.070 --> 00:17:48.430
And we can immediately extract
or glean some properties of it.

00:17:48.430 --> 00:17:55.800
So and in fact, maybe, yeah, so
let's look at some properties.

00:17:59.730 --> 00:18:06.080
Now, I'm going to totally
annoy many of you.

00:18:06.080 --> 00:18:08.520
Now, I'm going to continue here.

00:18:08.520 --> 00:18:10.330
So I'm going to
delete this part.

00:18:10.330 --> 00:18:10.830
Oh!

00:18:10.830 --> 00:18:14.220
I could actually continue here.

00:18:14.220 --> 00:18:16.170
OK, yeah, this is
going to be good.

00:18:16.170 --> 00:18:18.570
So then it'll be only for--

00:18:18.570 --> 00:18:21.030
Only half of that
whiteboard is visible.

00:18:21.030 --> 00:18:22.350
Oh, this part?

00:18:22.350 --> 00:18:23.820
Yeah, half of it is visible.

00:18:23.820 --> 00:18:24.810
Oh, this part.

00:18:24.810 --> 00:18:27.450
Yeah, pretty much.

00:18:27.450 --> 00:18:30.030
OK, let's see if I
can pull this off

00:18:30.030 --> 00:18:34.740
or if I can make use
of this real estate.

00:18:34.740 --> 00:18:37.050
After all, we're talking
about compression.

00:18:37.050 --> 00:18:40.650
We can't waste space.

00:18:40.650 --> 00:18:51.690
So let's look at one
alternative representation

00:18:51.690 --> 00:18:53.280
for mutual information.

00:18:53.280 --> 00:18:55.680
So let me play with this bit.

00:18:55.680 --> 00:19:00.690
So I'm looking at the
mutual information.

00:19:00.690 --> 00:19:08.690
And so it's equal to the
entropy of one of them

00:19:08.690 --> 00:19:15.620
plus the entropy of the other.

00:19:15.620 --> 00:19:27.870
Minus the joint entropy, and the
joint entropy as you've seen,

00:19:27.870 --> 00:19:32.180
one of the properties of
joint entropy you have seen

00:19:32.180 --> 00:19:41.510
is that it can be decomposed
to the entropy of one

00:19:41.510 --> 00:19:46.640
of them right plus the
conditional entropy

00:19:46.640 --> 00:19:47.375
of the other.

00:19:49.880 --> 00:19:54.320
So this is one way of
decomposing or representing

00:19:54.320 --> 00:19:56.540
the joint entropy.

00:19:56.540 --> 00:19:58.730
Everyone OK with this?

00:19:58.730 --> 00:20:00.800
Great.

00:20:00.800 --> 00:20:05.570
So now we're getting to--

00:20:05.570 --> 00:20:07.700
so these guys cancel.

00:20:07.700 --> 00:20:14.975
So I'm getting h of y
minus h of y given x.

00:20:18.910 --> 00:20:22.630
And similarly, I could
have decomposed this

00:20:22.630 --> 00:20:29.420
as, let's say, H of Y
plus H of X given Y.

00:20:29.420 --> 00:20:36.500
And then I have gotten that this
is the H of X minus H of X given

00:20:36.500 --> 00:20:41.660
Y.

00:20:41.660 --> 00:20:47.990
OK, so I guess
properties-- well,

00:20:47.990 --> 00:20:52.100
OK, a few things,
and this is a bit

00:20:52.100 --> 00:20:54.830
sorry for the mess in the order.

00:20:54.830 --> 00:20:58.310
But I guess one property
which is actually

00:20:58.310 --> 00:21:02.090
immediate from this
presentation is

00:21:02.090 --> 00:21:08.330
that it's symmetric in X
and Y, so mutual information

00:21:08.330 --> 00:21:15.350
between X and Y is equal to the
mutual information between Y

00:21:15.350 --> 00:21:20.990
and X. I guess one
way of seeing is

00:21:20.990 --> 00:21:24.890
from this from this presentation
because what are we seeing here.

00:21:24.890 --> 00:21:29.160
It's the entropy of one guy
plus the entropy of the other.

00:21:29.160 --> 00:21:32.760
So that's invariant to
the order between them

00:21:32.760 --> 00:21:36.990
minus their joint entropy.

00:21:36.990 --> 00:21:43.260
And it's also pretty, I
hope, self-evident that--

00:21:43.260 --> 00:21:47.580
because entropy really
is invariant to,

00:21:47.580 --> 00:21:51.270
let's say, relabeling or
the order of the elements

00:21:51.270 --> 00:21:52.200
of the alphabet.

00:21:52.200 --> 00:21:56.040
So the entropy of X and Y is
equal to the entropy of Y and X.

00:21:56.040 --> 00:21:59.460
It's really about
just essentially

00:21:59.460 --> 00:22:02.970
a different ordering
of the values

00:22:02.970 --> 00:22:06.480
that these pairs could take.

00:22:06.480 --> 00:22:09.570
So this two is invariant to
the order between X and Y,

00:22:09.570 --> 00:22:12.990
and therefore, this
whole thing is invariant.

00:22:12.990 --> 00:22:18.600
And then the second thing
which we can easily glean say

00:22:18.600 --> 00:22:22.500
from these representations,
taking this

00:22:22.500 --> 00:22:25.470
into account that
conditioning reduces entropy,

00:22:25.470 --> 00:22:31.636
is that the mutual information
is always non-negative.

00:22:35.110 --> 00:22:46.000
And a third property, which
I'm not going to get into,

00:22:46.000 --> 00:22:48.820
and maybe it'll be homework.

00:22:48.820 --> 00:22:53.080
Otherwise, you're just going to
have to wait for next quarter

00:22:53.080 --> 00:22:56.950
to take 376.

00:22:56.950 --> 00:23:00.700
And/or look at the
class notes from 376

00:23:00.700 --> 00:23:03.100
is that there is
another representation

00:23:03.100 --> 00:23:07.570
of mutual information which
personally I love most,

00:23:07.570 --> 00:23:13.030
and that is-- so after
a few additional steps,

00:23:13.030 --> 00:23:16.840
you can also get
to a representation

00:23:16.840 --> 00:23:20.230
of mutual information
as relative entropy.

00:23:20.230 --> 00:23:22.620
Remember relative entropy.

00:23:22.620 --> 00:23:27.310
Everyone remembers relative
entropy between two probability

00:23:27.310 --> 00:23:28.420
mass functions.

00:23:33.190 --> 00:23:36.920
Everyone remember that object.

00:23:36.920 --> 00:23:37.580
Great.

00:23:37.580 --> 00:23:44.600
So with a few lines
additional lines of work,

00:23:44.600 --> 00:23:47.720
you can also show that this is
equal to the relative entropy

00:23:47.720 --> 00:23:50.750
between the--

00:23:50.750 --> 00:23:53.530
I'll write it like this.

00:23:53.530 --> 00:23:57.670
The joint probability the
probability mass function that

00:23:57.670 --> 00:24:05.830
governs the pair X,Y and
the product probability mass

00:24:05.830 --> 00:24:06.370
function.

00:24:06.370 --> 00:24:14.410
So the probability mass function
on the pair X,Y when they each

00:24:14.410 --> 00:24:17.260
maintain their
marginal distribution,

00:24:17.260 --> 00:24:21.920
but they're independent.

00:24:21.920 --> 00:24:28.130
OK, so that's another
alternative representation

00:24:28.130 --> 00:24:29.420
of mutual information.

00:24:29.420 --> 00:24:36.140
And some of the reasons
I like it is number one.

00:24:36.140 --> 00:24:39.800
It gives you another, I
think, intuitive sense

00:24:39.800 --> 00:24:41.960
of what mutual information is.

00:24:41.960 --> 00:24:46.610
It's a measure of the
degree of dependence

00:24:46.610 --> 00:24:52.130
between the random variables.

00:24:52.130 --> 00:24:56.390
The more dependent
they are, the farther

00:24:56.390 --> 00:25:00.440
away their joint
probability mass function

00:25:00.440 --> 00:25:03.200
is from probability
mass function

00:25:03.200 --> 00:25:05.430
where they're independent.

00:25:05.430 --> 00:25:05.930
OK?

00:25:05.930 --> 00:25:08.720
And so the larger the
mutual information will be,

00:25:08.720 --> 00:25:12.740
and the more close they
are to independence.

00:25:12.740 --> 00:25:15.110
In fact, like when they're
independent, in fact,

00:25:15.110 --> 00:25:18.500
only when they're independent
will the mutual information be

00:25:18.500 --> 00:25:19.980
0.

00:25:19.980 --> 00:25:21.810
And this is one of
the ways of seeing it

00:25:21.810 --> 00:25:26.010
because only when
they're independent will

00:25:26.010 --> 00:25:30.860
their true joint probability
mass function have

00:25:30.860 --> 00:25:33.860
a relative entropy
zero with respect

00:25:33.860 --> 00:25:37.130
to the product
probability mass function.

00:25:37.130 --> 00:25:42.830
So oh, and then the other
reason why I love most,

00:25:42.830 --> 00:25:48.110
this representation, is
that it carries over.

00:25:48.110 --> 00:25:51.410
And on this again, you're going
to have to trust me for now,

00:25:51.410 --> 00:25:56.330
and maybe even for this
whole course, that even

00:25:56.330 --> 00:26:00.920
though everything we did
here was kind of assumed

00:26:00.920 --> 00:26:03.590
that x and y are discrete.

00:26:03.590 --> 00:26:07.700
So then it made sense to
talk about their entropies,

00:26:07.700 --> 00:26:12.020
their joint entropy,
their conditional entropy.

00:26:12.020 --> 00:26:16.940
But in fact, mutual
information carries over

00:26:16.940 --> 00:26:18.530
and is completely natural.

00:26:18.530 --> 00:26:21.140
So unlike entropy,
which doesn't make sense

00:26:21.140 --> 00:26:24.980
for continuous random
variables, mutual information

00:26:24.980 --> 00:26:31.380
makes total sense for
completely generally distributed

00:26:31.380 --> 00:26:33.310
random variables.

00:26:33.310 --> 00:26:36.815
So X,Y can take values in
whatever wherever you want.

00:26:36.815 --> 00:26:38.190
They can be
discrete, continuous,

00:26:38.190 --> 00:26:41.550
take values on the realign, take
values and abstract functional

00:26:41.550 --> 00:26:47.010
spaces, the whole sample
paths, whatever you want.

00:26:47.010 --> 00:26:51.870
Mutual information is still
well defined through, in fact,

00:26:51.870 --> 00:26:55.320
a very natural generalization
of relative entropy.

00:26:55.320 --> 00:26:59.310
You can always talk about the
relative entropy between two

00:26:59.310 --> 00:27:02.700
distributions, not necessarily
probability mass functions,

00:27:02.700 --> 00:27:06.330
but two distributions that
can govern arbitrarily

00:27:06.330 --> 00:27:10.530
abstract random objects.

00:27:10.530 --> 00:27:13.530
And so you can always talk
about a relative entropy

00:27:13.530 --> 00:27:15.930
between their joint
distribution and the product

00:27:15.930 --> 00:27:18.870
of their marginals.

00:27:18.870 --> 00:27:21.900
And again, those
who want the details

00:27:21.900 --> 00:27:27.720
will be motivated to come back
for more in the next quarter.

00:27:27.720 --> 00:27:32.053
And also, maybe to an
extent, some of this stuff,

00:27:32.053 --> 00:27:33.470
how, let's say,
mutual information

00:27:33.470 --> 00:27:38.720
extends to some forms of
continuous random variables,

00:27:38.720 --> 00:27:40.340
as the case might be.

00:27:40.340 --> 00:27:45.140
If and when we will
need it, we will

00:27:45.140 --> 00:27:51.410
have you guys work a little to
understand how things extend.

00:27:51.410 --> 00:27:55.340
But for today's discussion,
it's fine to just let's

00:27:55.340 --> 00:27:59.720
say initially just focus on
discrete random variables,

00:27:59.720 --> 00:28:05.060
and then and then at some point
just have some faith in me

00:28:05.060 --> 00:28:10.460
that this thing
extends to general also

00:28:10.460 --> 00:28:13.710
continuous, random variables.

00:28:13.710 --> 00:28:20.540
So any questions so far?

00:28:26.430 --> 00:28:29.380
And so now we're going to start.

00:28:29.380 --> 00:28:36.570
So how does this have anything
to do with lossy compression

00:28:36.570 --> 00:28:38.850
so far, just like with entropy.

00:28:38.850 --> 00:28:42.000
OK, if you didn't know,
you didn't take a course

00:28:42.000 --> 00:28:42.690
on compression.

00:28:42.690 --> 00:28:44.550
You'd look at this and
you'd say, OK, maybe

00:28:44.550 --> 00:28:46.008
there's some reason
to believe it's

00:28:46.008 --> 00:28:51.390
a nice measure of
some uncertainty.

00:28:51.390 --> 00:28:54.540
But then you realize
that, OK, it actually

00:28:54.540 --> 00:28:56.490
answers a very
fundamental question

00:28:56.490 --> 00:28:57.870
in lossless compression.

00:28:57.870 --> 00:28:59.700
So [? useful ?]
information, too,

00:28:59.700 --> 00:29:03.870
many people from
all sorts of fields

00:29:03.870 --> 00:29:10.020
just enjoy this as a nice
measure of dependence

00:29:10.020 --> 00:29:13.890
between random variables.

00:29:13.890 --> 00:29:16.530
And they use it for all
its nice properties.

00:29:16.530 --> 00:29:19.170
And there are several others
other nice properties,

00:29:19.170 --> 00:29:22.500
just a statistical
measure of dependence,

00:29:22.500 --> 00:29:28.300
that we might expose you
to in some of the homework.

00:29:28.300 --> 00:29:34.450
But much more
fundamentally, it arises

00:29:34.450 --> 00:29:41.380
in answering the very the most
basic fundamental question

00:29:41.380 --> 00:29:45.610
in lossy compression.

00:29:45.610 --> 00:29:49.840
OK, so let's remind
ourselves the general setting

00:29:49.840 --> 00:29:52.840
of lossless compression.

00:30:12.100 --> 00:30:19.090
OK, so let's say I have
n source symbols that I

00:30:19.090 --> 00:30:25.410
want to represent, x1, x2, xn.

00:30:30.580 --> 00:30:32.650
So I'm going to encode them.

00:30:32.650 --> 00:30:38.160
So I'm going to
design an encoder that

00:30:38.160 --> 00:30:47.070
represents this n-tuple and
hopefully not too many bits.

00:30:47.070 --> 00:30:54.420
So it's going to output some bit
representation of, let's say,

00:30:54.420 --> 00:30:55.515
m bits.

00:30:59.340 --> 00:31:08.880
And then, the idea will be
that a decoder, at some point,

00:31:08.880 --> 00:31:13.350
is going to read these, this
succinct representation of m

00:31:13.350 --> 00:31:19.810
bits and, from that, come
up with its reconstruction.

00:31:19.810 --> 00:31:23.170
And when we're talking about
lossless compression, then

00:31:23.170 --> 00:31:25.840
here the decoder was
required to come up

00:31:25.840 --> 00:31:31.420
with exactly the original data.

00:31:31.420 --> 00:31:36.100
So we would here require
exactly x1 to xn.

00:31:36.100 --> 00:31:42.400
But in the context of lossy
compression, we're OK.

00:31:42.400 --> 00:31:46.570
We might be OK if what
the decoder comes up

00:31:46.570 --> 00:31:50.140
with on the basis of this
compressed representation

00:31:50.140 --> 00:31:56.650
into m bits is something
else, hopefully,

00:31:56.650 --> 00:32:05.110
not too far off from this
source, from the original data.

00:32:05.110 --> 00:32:09.250
And so we usually also quantify.

00:32:09.250 --> 00:32:10.990
What does it mean
not too far off?

00:32:10.990 --> 00:32:17.290
Usually, you'll specify to me
a some distortion criterion,

00:32:17.290 --> 00:32:21.040
let's call it D, which--

00:32:21.040 --> 00:32:24.970
for today, it's going
to be interesting enough

00:32:24.970 --> 00:32:30.770
to consider as we often do
so-called component-wise or

00:32:30.770 --> 00:32:32.750
symbol-by-symbol
distortion criteria.

00:32:32.750 --> 00:32:38.060
So usually, you'll specify to
me a distortion between pairs,

00:32:38.060 --> 00:32:40.010
a distortion that
looks at the distortion

00:32:40.010 --> 00:32:45.650
between the i-th component
here and the i-th component

00:32:45.650 --> 00:32:49.430
here in the reconstruction.

00:32:49.430 --> 00:32:51.170
And then I'm going to look at--

00:32:51.170 --> 00:32:54.830
I'm going to judge the overall
goodness of this reconstruction

00:32:54.830 --> 00:33:04.790
according to, let's say,
the average, or if you will,

00:33:04.790 --> 00:33:07.070
the per symbol distortion.

00:33:10.480 --> 00:33:15.070
And, of course, this is,
as in the lossless case,

00:33:15.070 --> 00:33:19.690
we're going to be modeling
things probabilistically.

00:33:19.690 --> 00:33:20.680
This is going to be--

00:33:20.680 --> 00:33:23.050
this is going to come from
some probabilistic source.

00:33:23.050 --> 00:33:24.910
So this is a random variable.

00:33:24.910 --> 00:33:31.280
We're going to look
at its expectations.

00:33:31.280 --> 00:33:34.870
And this is what we're
going to colloquially

00:33:34.870 --> 00:33:38.590
referred to as the distortion.

00:33:38.590 --> 00:33:48.130
So this is the distortion
that your scheme achieves.

00:33:48.130 --> 00:33:53.780
If you want to be totally
elaborate and explicit,

00:33:53.780 --> 00:34:01.270
you're going to say, I guess,
the per symbol because it's

00:34:01.270 --> 00:34:02.380
normal--

00:34:02.380 --> 00:34:04.150
per symbol because
we look at the sum

00:34:04.150 --> 00:34:06.792
of all these individual
distortions and normalize.

00:34:06.792 --> 00:34:08.000
And then we take expectation.

00:34:08.000 --> 00:34:13.370
So per symbol,
expected distortion.

00:34:13.370 --> 00:34:19.790
But usually, we just talk
about the distortion achieved,

00:34:19.790 --> 00:34:22.670
and we mean this.

00:34:22.670 --> 00:34:24.139
And then, of
course, the question

00:34:24.139 --> 00:34:28.190
is, OK, obviously you
want the distortion

00:34:28.190 --> 00:34:30.920
to be as small as
possible, and of course--

00:34:30.920 --> 00:34:38.060
so distortion criteria that you
guys may you know some of you

00:34:38.060 --> 00:34:41.960
are familiar with is,
for example, Hamming.

00:34:41.960 --> 00:34:47.989
So let's say Hamming is a
distortion criterion that

00:34:47.989 --> 00:34:51.320
makes a lot of sense in the
context of discrete data.

00:34:51.320 --> 00:34:56.900
And then, we just
look at whether or not

00:34:56.900 --> 00:35:03.260
the reconstructed symbol is
equal to the original symbol.

00:35:03.260 --> 00:35:09.740
So it's an indicator for whether
X, the reconstruction symbol,

00:35:09.740 --> 00:35:11.980
is--

00:35:11.980 --> 00:35:15.250
if the reconstruction symbol is
equal to the original symbol,

00:35:15.250 --> 00:35:16.660
then there is 0 loss.

00:35:16.660 --> 00:35:22.480
And otherwise, we
count it as a loss.

00:35:22.480 --> 00:35:32.650
So that's called-- so examples
of distortion criteria.

00:35:36.730 --> 00:35:45.070
So this is one, and this is
called Hamming distortion.

00:35:45.070 --> 00:35:50.020
And then, as we as we
said, lossy compression

00:35:50.020 --> 00:35:54.760
is also, in fact, in some ways
even more much more interesting

00:35:54.760 --> 00:35:59.290
in the context of
continuous data

00:35:59.290 --> 00:36:04.870
where you have no choice
but to do lossy compression.

00:36:04.870 --> 00:36:10.840
And then, of course, let's
say if these are real-valued,

00:36:10.840 --> 00:36:13.210
these are, I don't know,
sensor measurements,

00:36:13.210 --> 00:36:15.880
these are real-valued, then
it makes a lot of sense

00:36:15.880 --> 00:36:20.500
to look at something like
squared error distortion.

00:36:32.060 --> 00:36:36.710
OK, so usually,
in an application,

00:36:36.710 --> 00:36:39.980
you'll tell me
what is the right--

00:36:39.980 --> 00:36:43.400
what's the most relevant
distortion criteria?

00:36:43.400 --> 00:36:49.640
Let's say if this is, let's
say, genomic data, these

00:36:49.640 --> 00:36:54.830
are nucleotides taking values in
a space of size four, and your--

00:36:54.830 --> 00:36:57.950
then it might make a lot of
sense to talk about Hamming

00:36:57.950 --> 00:37:01.610
and to tell me, yeah, I'm
willing to suffer a bit

00:37:01.610 --> 00:37:04.370
of Hamming distortion, positive
Hamming distortion meaning

00:37:04.370 --> 00:37:07.880
the reconstruction, every once
in a while you're going to get

00:37:07.880 --> 00:37:13.220
a base pair incorrect if it's--
if it means that we're going

00:37:13.220 --> 00:37:18.830
to need way less bits here in
the representation than we would

00:37:18.830 --> 00:37:23.627
if I required totally lossless
compression, in which case,

00:37:23.627 --> 00:37:24.710
we would need the entropy.

00:37:24.710 --> 00:37:28.940
So maybe now, if I'm willing
to suffer some positive Hamming

00:37:28.940 --> 00:37:32.715
distortion, maybe we can
get away with less bits.

00:37:36.420 --> 00:37:37.680
OK, so there's the distortion.

00:37:37.680 --> 00:37:39.097
And then the
question is, OK, what

00:37:39.097 --> 00:37:41.490
does it mean getting
away with less bits?

00:37:41.490 --> 00:37:44.340
So concretely, we
talk about the rate

00:37:44.340 --> 00:37:47.430
just as we did in
lossless compression.

00:37:47.430 --> 00:37:50.220
We had the compression
rate, which

00:37:50.220 --> 00:37:56.460
was the ratio
between the, I guess,

00:37:56.460 --> 00:37:59.940
the number of bits in the
compressor presentation

00:37:59.940 --> 00:38:04.260
and the overall length
of the sequence.

00:38:04.260 --> 00:38:08.670
So it was like, How many bits do
I need per sequence component?

00:38:08.670 --> 00:38:10.470
So that was the rate.

00:38:10.470 --> 00:38:12.630
And so here, too,
that's the rate.

00:38:12.630 --> 00:38:20.940
It's going to be the ratio
between the number of bits, m,

00:38:20.940 --> 00:38:24.150
that my compressor
presentation is requiring.

00:38:24.150 --> 00:38:30.360
And in how many overall source
components that I managed

00:38:30.360 --> 00:38:33.810
to represent with these m bits.

00:38:33.810 --> 00:38:36.780
So here is like n
source components.

00:38:36.780 --> 00:38:42.030
OK, so that's the
rate in bits per--

00:38:42.030 --> 00:38:46.890
usually, you write
bits per source symbol,

00:38:46.890 --> 00:38:52.000
or we just say bits per symbol.

00:38:52.000 --> 00:38:54.700
OK, and we know, again,
lossless compression--

00:38:54.700 --> 00:38:58.120
we know that the
best that you can do

00:38:58.120 --> 00:39:03.580
is in terms of rate if you want
this to actually equal this

00:39:03.580 --> 00:39:06.700
or in terms of
distortion if you want,

00:39:06.700 --> 00:39:10.900
let's say, the Hamming
distortion to be 0,

00:39:10.900 --> 00:39:13.690
if you want this
thing to be 0, then

00:39:13.690 --> 00:39:18.430
we know what is the minimum
rate I'm going to need.

00:39:18.430 --> 00:39:19.630
That's the entropy.

00:39:19.630 --> 00:39:22.690
Let's say if these are IID
according to some distribution

00:39:22.690 --> 00:39:26.200
X. It's going to be the
distribution the entropy of X.

00:39:26.200 --> 00:39:31.530
But the question is now if
I allow some distortion.

00:39:31.530 --> 00:39:35.940
OK, so maybe we can talk--

00:39:35.940 --> 00:39:39.090
so here's my question.

00:39:39.090 --> 00:39:46.035
And I'm not going to be
maximally formal about it.

00:39:49.350 --> 00:39:51.690
And I'm just going
to write it in words.

00:39:51.690 --> 00:39:56.340
So the question is,
what is the minimum rate

00:39:56.340 --> 00:40:15.040
needed for getting distortion
no more than some specified

00:40:15.040 --> 00:40:20.440
distortion level that
you're willing to tolerate?

00:40:24.960 --> 00:40:28.170
OK, so again,
lossless compression,

00:40:28.170 --> 00:40:29.280
we've answered this.

00:40:29.280 --> 00:40:33.810
If this D is 0,
what's the min rate

00:40:33.810 --> 00:40:36.765
that we need for
distortion zero?

00:40:39.630 --> 00:40:43.860
And the chorus sings--

00:40:43.860 --> 00:40:45.120
entropy.

00:40:45.120 --> 00:40:47.160
Exactly.

00:40:47.160 --> 00:40:51.960
But what is the minimum rate
needed if I'm willing to suffer?

00:40:51.960 --> 00:40:56.820
Let's say we're doing Hamming
loss, and this d is, let's say,

00:40:56.820 --> 00:41:07.110
0.1, meaning having lost
D0.1, meaning I'm OK with this

00:41:07.110 --> 00:41:13.530
reconstruction, getting only
90% of the symbols correctly.

00:41:13.530 --> 00:41:18.210
Then, we should expect
to be able to represent

00:41:18.210 --> 00:41:22.800
the data with less bits
to have a compression

00:41:22.800 --> 00:41:29.070
rate smaller than the entropy.

00:41:29.070 --> 00:41:33.960
And this question was
answered beautifully,

00:41:33.960 --> 00:41:41.400
crisply, and quantitatively
by Shannon, of course.

00:41:41.400 --> 00:41:48.810
So Shannon's theorem
in this context,

00:41:48.810 --> 00:41:51.990
or one of Shannon's
theorem, so in this context

00:41:51.990 --> 00:42:02.430
of lossy compression,
is that the--

00:42:02.430 --> 00:42:05.370
OK, let me also denote.

00:42:05.370 --> 00:42:23.020
So let's denote the
answer by R of D.

00:42:23.020 --> 00:42:29.080
So what is the
minimum rate needed

00:42:29.080 --> 00:42:34.030
for achieving the distortion
no more than D. OK,

00:42:34.030 --> 00:42:36.070
when I allow you
to optimize over

00:42:36.070 --> 00:42:38.500
all schemes in the
world, arbitrarily

00:42:38.500 --> 00:42:45.790
complex, sophisticated, using
arbitrarily large blocks of data

00:42:45.790 --> 00:42:51.970
simultaneously, what is
the minimum, the best

00:42:51.970 --> 00:42:54.820
that you can do, optimizing
over all of these?

00:42:54.820 --> 00:42:58.240
OK, so there's some answer,
there's some minimum rate

00:42:58.240 --> 00:43:04.910
that we're going to need
denoted by this R of D.

00:43:04.910 --> 00:43:09.860
And Shannon's theorem is that R
of D, the minimum rate that you

00:43:09.860 --> 00:43:17.750
need to achieve
distortion, no more than D,

00:43:17.750 --> 00:43:24.290
is given by the minimum
mutual information between X

00:43:24.290 --> 00:43:28.850
and Y. Oh, OK, I should say--
so this was very general.

00:43:28.850 --> 00:43:31.430
I didn't talk about what
the distribution of the data

00:43:31.430 --> 00:43:32.090
might be.

00:43:32.090 --> 00:43:37.220
So here, again, to be concrete,
just like in the context

00:43:37.220 --> 00:43:39.890
of lossless compression,
we talked initially

00:43:39.890 --> 00:43:43.910
about IID sources
of information.

00:43:43.910 --> 00:43:48.110
So here, too, so Shannon's
theorem for IID source--

00:43:48.110 --> 00:44:00.480
so for if the XIs are IID
according to some generic X--

00:44:00.480 --> 00:44:02.910
just like in
lossless compression,

00:44:02.910 --> 00:44:06.450
we know if the XIs are IID
according to some generic X,

00:44:06.450 --> 00:44:09.840
then the minimum rate that you
need for lossless compression

00:44:09.840 --> 00:44:11.880
is the entropy of X.

00:44:11.880 --> 00:44:15.450
So here, let's again
assume the XIs are IID

00:44:15.450 --> 00:44:19.860
according to X then what's
the minimum rate that you

00:44:19.860 --> 00:44:24.090
need if you're allowing
distortion up to d?

00:44:24.090 --> 00:44:27.390
It's the minimum mutual
information between this generic

00:44:27.390 --> 00:44:31.110
X that represents the
distribution of your source

00:44:31.110 --> 00:44:36.060
components and a generic
reconstruction variable y,

00:44:36.060 --> 00:44:42.690
minimized overall joint
distributions on X,Y such that

00:44:42.690 --> 00:44:49.290
the expected distortion
between X,Y is no more than d.

00:44:59.270 --> 00:45:04.940
OK, so just to be
slightly more explicit,

00:45:04.940 --> 00:45:11.690
again, the distribution of
the source of these XIs, X,

00:45:11.690 --> 00:45:12.770
is given to you.

00:45:12.770 --> 00:45:15.110
That's not something
you can control.

00:45:15.110 --> 00:45:18.140
That's the characterization,
the characteristic

00:45:18.140 --> 00:45:20.600
of your source of information.

00:45:20.600 --> 00:45:24.170
Your XIs are IIDX.

00:45:24.170 --> 00:45:29.240
So this minimization is really
over all joint distributions

00:45:29.240 --> 00:45:34.040
on X and Y, but consistent with
the given distribution on X.

00:45:34.040 --> 00:45:37.670
So this is really
a minimization over

00:45:37.670 --> 00:45:43.790
the conditional distribution
on Y given X, basically

00:45:43.790 --> 00:45:46.700
over all conditional
distributions of Y

00:45:46.700 --> 00:45:52.040
given X that satisfy this
constraint under which

00:45:52.040 --> 00:45:59.210
the expected distortion between
X and Y is no more than D. OK,

00:45:59.210 --> 00:46:01.580
so as it should be,
like in the end,

00:46:01.580 --> 00:46:10.430
this is some functional of just
the distribution of X, really.

00:46:10.430 --> 00:46:14.120
We're optimizing
over what remains

00:46:14.120 --> 00:46:17.000
of the joint distribution,
which is the conditional of P

00:46:17.000 --> 00:46:20.750
of Y given X.

00:46:20.750 --> 00:46:28.190
What else do I want to say
about it at this point?

00:46:28.190 --> 00:46:33.080
Maybe the best thing is
to basically go straight

00:46:33.080 --> 00:46:36.600
to two examples.

00:46:36.600 --> 00:46:39.530
So two examples
corresponding respectively

00:46:39.530 --> 00:46:41.120
to these two
distortion criteria.

00:46:51.720 --> 00:46:54.630
And by the way, everything
that I'm doing today

00:46:54.630 --> 00:47:00.570
will be done in more detail
and more rigor, but not

00:47:00.570 --> 00:47:09.000
pedantically, but still slightly
more detail and rigor in 376.

00:47:09.000 --> 00:47:15.960
And so let's look
at one example,

00:47:15.960 --> 00:47:19.680
or let's say the
first example is

00:47:19.680 --> 00:47:24.570
going to be the rate-distortion
function associated

00:47:24.570 --> 00:47:31.950
with or for a Bernoulli source.

00:47:37.590 --> 00:47:42.930
OK, so in the case of
Bernoulli source, in the end,

00:47:42.930 --> 00:47:47.940
this is a very wieldy
finite-dimensional problem.

00:47:47.940 --> 00:47:51.360
In fact, the distribution
on x, if x is--

00:47:54.060 --> 00:47:56.730
so here I'm going to assume
so more specifically.

00:47:56.730 --> 00:48:05.205
So X is Bernoulli of
some parameter small p.

00:48:09.180 --> 00:48:12.090
And let's assume without
loss of generality

00:48:12.090 --> 00:48:16.560
that this p is no more than 1/2.

00:48:16.560 --> 00:48:20.940
Otherwise, we can flip it
and things will carry over

00:48:20.940 --> 00:48:22.140
to the complement.

00:48:25.740 --> 00:48:27.240
And as we said--

00:48:27.240 --> 00:48:32.880
and now let's look at
the distortion criterion

00:48:32.880 --> 00:48:33.840
being Hamming.

00:48:41.410 --> 00:48:49.750
OK, so in the end, for this
problem, solving for this R of D

00:48:49.750 --> 00:48:54.520
is just a very small
low-dimensional optimization

00:48:54.520 --> 00:48:59.170
problem because Y here, the
reconstruction variable,

00:48:59.170 --> 00:49:02.620
is obviously also binary.

00:49:02.620 --> 00:49:05.680
So again, here,
this is the source.

00:49:05.680 --> 00:49:11.110
These information components
are just IID Bernoulli p.

00:49:11.110 --> 00:49:13.870
Just coin flips
with some bias p.

00:49:13.870 --> 00:49:15.790
And so the
reconstruction is also

00:49:15.790 --> 00:49:19.960
going to be obviously
with binary.

00:49:19.960 --> 00:49:26.140
And so this simply
boils down to optimizing

00:49:26.140 --> 00:49:31.690
for a conditional distribution
of one binary variable y given

00:49:31.690 --> 00:49:33.730
another binary variable x.

00:49:33.730 --> 00:49:40.000
So even if you don't accommodate
all sorts of symmetries

00:49:40.000 --> 00:49:44.500
that are here, it's a very
small number of parameters.

00:49:44.500 --> 00:49:48.460
It's at most-- what's
it going to be?

00:49:48.460 --> 00:49:52.210
Maybe two parameters for
this optimization problem,

00:49:52.210 --> 00:49:54.340
but there's also--

00:49:54.340 --> 00:49:58.750
you can also exploit the
fact that there's symmetry.

00:49:58.750 --> 00:50:02.530
And so again, if
we had more time,

00:50:02.530 --> 00:50:04.930
and we're going to
do this in next order

00:50:04.930 --> 00:50:10.660
and you can look at the notes,
you could easily do the math

00:50:10.660 --> 00:50:17.770
and get to the fact that
for this setting, R of D

00:50:17.770 --> 00:50:22.130
is simply this.

00:50:22.130 --> 00:50:25.600
So it's the binary
entropy associated

00:50:25.600 --> 00:50:31.090
with the source, so the
entropy of the source,

00:50:31.090 --> 00:50:36.520
minus the binary
entropy evaluated at D,

00:50:36.520 --> 00:50:42.140
the distortion level at
the interesting region

00:50:42.140 --> 00:50:46.430
where the distortion
is between 0 and p,

00:50:46.430 --> 00:50:51.710
and 0 for distortion
more than p.

00:50:54.710 --> 00:50:56.720
Guys, can you remind me or--

00:50:56.720 --> 00:51:01.970
have we already introduced this
notation or the binary entropy?

00:51:01.970 --> 00:51:04.790
[INAUDIBLE] one time, but--

00:51:04.790 --> 00:51:16.250
OK, so just in case when
I write H2 of, say q,

00:51:16.250 --> 00:51:21.320
I just mean the entropy of,
I don't know, let's call it

00:51:21.320 --> 00:51:31.670
Z, for Z Bernoulli
variable of parameter q.

00:51:38.810 --> 00:51:41.630
OK, so this is what we--

00:51:41.630 --> 00:51:46.210
so for this, we're calling it
the binary entropy function,

00:51:46.210 --> 00:51:51.750
and I think you've even
seen what it looks like.

00:51:51.750 --> 00:51:57.380
And so, in fact, if you
need a quick reminder,

00:51:57.380 --> 00:52:01.430
the binary entropy function,
what does it look like?

00:52:01.430 --> 00:52:03.590
H2, let's say q--

00:52:07.350 --> 00:52:12.870
it's around a 1/2, and it
does something like this.

00:52:16.410 --> 00:52:19.650
And this corresponds to the
maximal value corresponding

00:52:19.650 --> 00:52:24.000
to the entropy of a Bernoulli
half variable, a fair coin

00:52:24.000 --> 00:52:28.785
flip, which is 1.

00:52:34.950 --> 00:52:37.370
So that's a binary
entropy function.

00:52:37.370 --> 00:52:39.310
And so the
rate-distortion function

00:52:39.310 --> 00:52:41.980
here-- let's so let's see what
the rate-distortion function

00:52:41.980 --> 00:52:43.900
here boils down to.

00:52:43.900 --> 00:52:46.660
Let's see, it makes sense.

00:52:50.110 --> 00:52:54.130
It starts, so here
now I'm plotting

00:52:54.130 --> 00:53:00.970
R of D, the function of d.

00:53:05.980 --> 00:53:07.220
Let's see.

00:53:07.220 --> 00:53:15.010
So at D equals 0, I
have H2 of 0 is 0.

00:53:15.010 --> 00:53:19.000
So I have-- so I
start right here

00:53:19.000 --> 00:53:26.240
at binary entropy of p, which
is the entropy of my source.

00:53:26.240 --> 00:53:30.500
So that's where I start.

00:53:30.500 --> 00:53:35.560
And then I get all the
way to distortion p,

00:53:35.560 --> 00:53:41.635
where at distortion p, I
have this minus H2 of also p.

00:53:41.635 --> 00:53:43.880
That's 0.

00:53:43.880 --> 00:53:47.770
All right so I'm taking
this constant value

00:53:47.770 --> 00:53:53.050
and subtracting from
it like this thing

00:53:53.050 --> 00:53:57.250
until I get to value p.

00:53:57.250 --> 00:53:59.455
And at p I'm going to get to 0.

00:54:02.770 --> 00:54:05.330
And then starting
from that point.

00:54:05.330 --> 00:54:06.335
It's going to be 0.

00:54:10.260 --> 00:54:15.720
So let's see, this
it makes sense,

00:54:15.720 --> 00:54:18.000
at least some sanity checks.

00:54:18.000 --> 00:54:23.790
So at distortion zero,
lossless compression, what's

00:54:23.790 --> 00:54:26.220
the minimum rate
I'm going to need,

00:54:26.220 --> 00:54:29.010
the minimum number of bits per
source symbol I'm going to need?

00:54:29.010 --> 00:54:32.310
Well, we know it's
the source entropy.

00:54:32.310 --> 00:54:35.910
And the source entropy is
indeed the binary entropy

00:54:35.910 --> 00:54:39.480
of p, the Bernoulli
parameter of the source.

00:54:39.480 --> 00:54:44.550
So that corresponds
to distortion zero,

00:54:44.550 --> 00:54:48.810
obviously starting from
distortion p and on.

00:54:48.810 --> 00:54:53.430
I don't need to tell you
anything about the source.

00:54:53.430 --> 00:54:56.220
I don't need any bits
of a presentation

00:54:56.220 --> 00:55:04.200
here because I'm just going to
reconstruct with, let's say,

00:55:04.200 --> 00:55:04.860
all zeros.

00:55:04.860 --> 00:55:07.440
My reconstruction is
going to be all zeros.

00:55:07.440 --> 00:55:14.400
And so, on average, my
expected Hamming loss

00:55:14.400 --> 00:55:23.090
is going to be p because,
again, my source is going to be

00:55:23.090 --> 00:55:25.250
1 only with probability p.

00:55:25.250 --> 00:55:28.790
So with probability
1 minus p, I'm

00:55:28.790 --> 00:55:32.990
going to get it right on
each of these components.

00:55:32.990 --> 00:55:36.147
So my expected distortion
is going to be p.

00:55:36.147 --> 00:55:38.480
If I just have reconstructions,
all zeros, and for that,

00:55:38.480 --> 00:55:42.050
I don't need you to tell me
anything about the source.

00:55:42.050 --> 00:55:45.440
So that's this part.

00:55:45.440 --> 00:55:50.990
Another thing that we're
seeing here, which is,

00:55:50.990 --> 00:55:54.470
in fact, not
coincidental, and it's

00:55:54.470 --> 00:55:58.100
true of all rate-distortion
functions for any source,

00:55:58.100 --> 00:56:01.730
is this convexity.

00:56:01.730 --> 00:56:07.640
And maybe one, not to belabor
this point too much for lack

00:56:07.640 --> 00:56:15.770
of time, but let's, for example,
look at this midpoint here.

00:56:15.770 --> 00:56:18.605
Let's see, corresponding
to distortion, p/2.

00:56:22.010 --> 00:56:24.530
And so let's look at--

00:56:24.530 --> 00:56:31.310
let's say this, the half of the
entropy of the source entropy.

00:56:31.310 --> 00:56:34.700
Let's look at this.

00:56:34.700 --> 00:56:41.870
So basically, this point
right here, OK, this point

00:56:41.870 --> 00:56:50.990
corresponds to p/2 and
binary entropy of p/2.

00:56:53.840 --> 00:57:02.210
Why would-- should this
point be above the value

00:57:02.210 --> 00:57:05.030
of the rate-distortion function?

00:57:05.030 --> 00:57:10.160
Because this point corresponds
to something very trivial

00:57:10.160 --> 00:57:11.570
that you can do.

00:57:11.570 --> 00:57:15.350
You can just decide that,
OK, half of the data,

00:57:15.350 --> 00:57:21.280
like the first half, I'm
going to describe losslessly.

00:57:21.280 --> 00:57:26.890
And so I'm going to need
entropy, bits per symbol,

00:57:26.890 --> 00:57:31.090
but it's only going to
be half of the data.

00:57:31.090 --> 00:57:33.340
So in amortized
over the full data,

00:57:33.340 --> 00:57:37.900
I'm only going to use these
many bits per source symbol.

00:57:40.750 --> 00:57:42.490
And then the other
half, I'm not going

00:57:42.490 --> 00:57:44.620
to tell you anything
about, and just guess,

00:57:44.620 --> 00:57:46.510
so reconstruct it
with all zeros.

00:57:46.510 --> 00:57:50.950
So on the other half, you're
going to get, as we discussed,

00:57:50.950 --> 00:57:52.840
average distortion
p, but it's only

00:57:52.840 --> 00:57:54.520
going to be on 1/2 of the data.

00:57:54.520 --> 00:57:57.820
So overall, amortized
on the full data,

00:57:57.820 --> 00:58:00.820
you're going to have
this much distortion.

00:58:00.820 --> 00:58:04.300
OK, so this point
right here corresponds

00:58:04.300 --> 00:58:07.900
to a stupid time-sharing
scheme, if you will,

00:58:07.900 --> 00:58:11.665
that timeshares between
these two extreme points.

00:58:17.510 --> 00:58:21.620
But obviously, there are
smarter things that can be done,

00:58:21.620 --> 00:58:26.070
and those smarter things will
get you all the way here.

00:58:26.070 --> 00:58:29.600
And what are these
smarter things?

00:58:29.600 --> 00:58:39.020
I hope to give you a bit of an
intuition with the next example.

00:58:39.020 --> 00:58:42.410
I should be striving
for allowing you

00:58:42.410 --> 00:58:45.080
about 10 minutes at
the end, which means I

00:58:45.080 --> 00:58:48.620
have order of 11 more minutes.

00:58:48.620 --> 00:58:49.670
You can [INAUDIBLE].

00:58:49.670 --> 00:58:51.290
OK, thank you.

00:58:51.290 --> 00:58:54.140
Very generous.

00:58:54.140 --> 00:59:05.627
OK, so is everybody more
or less good with the main,

00:59:05.627 --> 00:59:06.710
the essence of this story?

00:59:06.710 --> 00:59:09.900
I know that there are I
didn't go into all the details

00:59:09.900 --> 00:59:13.985
but feel free to ask
me anything about this.

00:59:21.900 --> 00:59:26.280
OK, so while you think about
what you may want to ask,

00:59:26.280 --> 00:59:31.020
let's continue here.

00:59:31.020 --> 00:59:33.765
Let's look at example two.

00:59:37.930 --> 00:59:45.640
So example two corresponds
to a Gaussian source.

00:59:45.640 --> 00:59:55.090
So here, X, let's say,
is standard Gaussian.

00:59:55.090 --> 00:59:57.025
So now your source
components X1,

00:59:57.025 --> 01:00:01.180
X2 are IID standard Gaussian.

01:00:01.180 --> 01:00:07.030
And the distortion criteria,
criterion, we're using

01:00:07.030 --> 01:00:08.110
is squared error.

01:00:12.310 --> 01:00:14.770
OK, so your source components
are now real-valued.

01:00:14.770 --> 01:00:18.370
Also, the reconstruction
components

01:00:18.370 --> 01:00:21.340
here are naturally
going to be real-valued.

01:00:21.340 --> 01:00:23.890
So it makes a lot of
sense to, for example,

01:00:23.890 --> 01:00:28.240
look at the squared error.

01:00:28.240 --> 01:00:36.590
And for this problem,
the miracle so happens.

01:00:36.590 --> 01:00:39.700
And again, maybe some of it
will give you a taste for.

01:00:39.700 --> 01:00:43.720
Otherwise, as usual,
wait for next quarter.

01:00:43.720 --> 01:00:47.050
The miracle also happens that
even though this is ostensibly

01:00:47.050 --> 01:00:51.190
now an infinite-dimensional
optimization problem,

01:00:51.190 --> 01:00:58.840
because now we need to optimize
over conditional PDFS of Y,

01:00:58.840 --> 01:01:03.550
which is a real value
random variable given X-- so

01:01:03.550 --> 01:01:07.600
that's an infinitely
dimensional object.

01:01:07.600 --> 01:01:13.530
But you can actually solve
this thing very explicitly.

01:01:13.530 --> 01:01:16.480
And it's, in fact,
not hard, not hard

01:01:16.480 --> 01:01:24.100
to do due to some properties
of the Gaussian distribution

01:01:24.100 --> 01:01:26.830
and how well it gels
with squared error

01:01:26.830 --> 01:01:30.790
distortion and some
maximum entropy properties

01:01:30.790 --> 01:01:36.820
that we will get into
in the next quarter.

01:01:36.820 --> 01:01:41.202
And so you bring these
properties together,

01:01:41.202 --> 01:01:42.910
and you solve this,
and you get that it's

01:01:42.910 --> 01:01:53.560
equal to 1/2 log of
1/d where it matters

01:01:53.560 --> 01:02:03.250
for the interesting range of
D, no more than 1 and then 0.

01:02:11.880 --> 01:02:14.940
OK, so let's see that this is--

01:02:14.940 --> 01:02:19.267
that this makes sense,
or let's look at the--

01:02:19.267 --> 01:02:20.475
let's see what it looks like.

01:02:39.700 --> 01:02:41.680
OK, let me plot it here.

01:02:41.680 --> 01:02:43.000
It looks something like--

01:02:48.120 --> 01:02:55.390
here's R of D. Here's
D. And here's 1.

01:02:55.390 --> 01:03:00.230
And starting from 1, it's 0.

01:03:00.230 --> 01:03:05.405
And then, for the less than 1,
it's doing something like this.

01:03:12.760 --> 01:03:19.530
And so let's see that it
makes sense for D more than 1,

01:03:19.530 --> 01:03:22.080
again, the same
kind of argument.

01:03:22.080 --> 01:03:27.720
The variance of
your source is one.

01:03:27.720 --> 01:03:33.030
Then, to get distortion squared,
error, distortion per symbol

01:03:33.030 --> 01:03:36.390
no more than 1, I don't
need to know anything.

01:03:36.390 --> 01:03:38.880
Don't give me any
information, any bits.

01:03:38.880 --> 01:03:42.150
Again, my reconstruction
is going to be all zeros.

01:03:42.150 --> 01:03:45.780
And then, the
expected distortion

01:03:45.780 --> 01:03:49.980
between the actual realized
value and my reconstruction

01:03:49.980 --> 01:03:53.670
is going to be the
variance of the source 1.

01:03:53.670 --> 01:03:56.250
So I don't need any
bits of information

01:03:56.250 --> 01:04:01.730
about the actual realization of
your source to get distortion 1.

01:04:08.070 --> 01:04:10.320
But if you want
something non-trivial,

01:04:10.320 --> 01:04:13.980
you want smaller and
smaller distortion,

01:04:13.980 --> 01:04:18.750
we need more and more
bits per source symbol.

01:04:18.750 --> 01:04:23.100
And as Pulkit discussed in the
previous lecture, obviously

01:04:23.100 --> 01:04:26.670
here we're talking
about X, which is

01:04:26.670 --> 01:04:29.970
a continuous random variable.

01:04:29.970 --> 01:04:34.770
And so first, in the sense we
can't talk about its entropy.

01:04:34.770 --> 01:04:36.600
Its entropy is
obviously infinite.

01:04:36.600 --> 01:04:41.130
So that's one way of
understanding why, this would--

01:04:41.130 --> 01:04:44.640
for a discrete source
at distortion zero,

01:04:44.640 --> 01:04:46.230
this would be the entropy.

01:04:46.230 --> 01:04:48.810
But now, it's a
continuous source.

01:04:48.810 --> 01:04:50.100
It has an infinite entropy.

01:04:50.100 --> 01:04:52.620
And obviously, if we--

01:04:52.620 --> 01:04:56.850
as sophisticated as we try
to be, if we want to get more

01:04:56.850 --> 01:05:00.240
and more fine with describing
the source components,

01:05:00.240 --> 01:05:03.540
we're going to need to
put in more and more bits

01:05:03.540 --> 01:05:05.790
of representation
for source symbol

01:05:05.790 --> 01:05:09.810
because, again,
it's a real value.

01:05:09.810 --> 01:05:12.720
You're going to need infinitely
many bits of precision

01:05:12.720 --> 01:05:15.390
if you really want
to actually nail it.

01:05:18.390 --> 01:05:24.240
And by the way, you can also--

01:05:24.240 --> 01:05:28.215
maybe I'll mention it that
as you can imagine, just

01:05:28.215 --> 01:05:30.090
like we can talk about
rate distortion, which

01:05:30.090 --> 01:05:32.880
is the minimum rate
you need if you require

01:05:32.880 --> 01:05:36.210
a certain level of distortion,
you can also flip this,

01:05:36.210 --> 01:05:41.220
and similarly you can talk about
the distortion rate function,

01:05:41.220 --> 01:05:47.880
what's the minimum
distortion that I can achieve

01:05:47.880 --> 01:05:52.620
if I'm allowed to dedicate no
more than R bits per source

01:05:52.620 --> 01:05:53.550
symbol?

01:05:53.550 --> 01:05:58.590
And so you can easily
flip this function,

01:05:58.590 --> 01:06:00.850
and you're going to see--

01:06:00.850 --> 01:06:03.480
and you can do the
exercise, and you're

01:06:03.480 --> 01:06:08.750
going to see that
it's this much.

01:06:08.750 --> 01:06:14.620
OK, so this is the, again,
the squared error, the average

01:06:14.620 --> 01:06:20.580
or the mean squared error
per symbol that you can get,

01:06:20.580 --> 01:06:22.890
the absolute
minimum that you can

01:06:22.890 --> 01:06:30.120
get if you were to optimize
overall schemes in the world,

01:06:30.120 --> 01:06:33.660
arbitrarily sophisticated.

01:06:33.660 --> 01:06:35.580
It's basically
flipping this thing.

01:06:35.580 --> 01:06:37.080
So D of R here.

01:06:40.730 --> 01:06:44.030
So D of R for this
source would start

01:06:44.030 --> 01:06:51.960
at 1, obviously with the
raid zero bits per symbol.

01:06:51.960 --> 01:06:53.730
OK, I'm going to get
a mean squared error

01:06:53.730 --> 01:06:56.730
of 1, the source of variance.

01:06:56.730 --> 01:07:03.180
But then, the more bits
I'm willing to expend

01:07:03.180 --> 01:07:06.840
per source symbol, the
smaller my distortion.

01:07:06.840 --> 01:07:12.390
And so this is going to be--
this thing is 2 to the minus 2R,

01:07:12.390 --> 01:07:14.010
which--

01:07:14.010 --> 01:07:16.590
I don't know if now is
the time to get into it,

01:07:16.590 --> 01:07:21.130
but, and maybe we'll
have a homework exercise.

01:07:21.130 --> 01:07:26.230
But Pulkit, did you last time
actually compute, let's say,

01:07:26.230 --> 01:07:30.670
how much distortion you're
getting with-- let's say

01:07:30.670 --> 01:07:38.080
you're looking at one bit
per for Gaussian component,

01:07:38.080 --> 01:07:40.960
but with scalar quantization?

01:07:40.960 --> 01:07:43.420
What was the distortion
that you got?

01:07:43.420 --> 01:07:45.165
Did you actually
do the computation?

01:07:45.165 --> 01:07:46.540
We didn't compute
the distortion,

01:07:46.540 --> 01:07:50.050
but we computed the codewords.

01:07:50.050 --> 01:07:52.510
OK, so you could do the math.

01:07:52.510 --> 01:07:59.990
So let's say at rate
equal 1, meaning

01:07:59.990 --> 01:08:03.730
I'm willing to dedicate one bit
of a presentation per source

01:08:03.730 --> 01:08:05.410
symbol.

01:08:05.410 --> 01:08:10.180
Then, you could do something
simple like Pulkit,

01:08:10.180 --> 01:08:13.420
like you guys went over
with Pulkit, such as just

01:08:13.420 --> 01:08:17.979
for every source symbol,
separately, just give me one bit

01:08:17.979 --> 01:08:20.750
to tell me if it's
positive or negative.

01:08:20.750 --> 01:08:23.810
OK, so it's going to be
one bit per source symbol.

01:08:23.810 --> 01:08:26.330
So that's going to
correspond to rate 1.

01:08:26.330 --> 01:08:28.939
And you can do the
math for that scheme.

01:08:28.939 --> 01:08:31.189
What was the
distortion that it got?

01:08:31.189 --> 01:08:35.000
And it got something.

01:08:35.000 --> 01:08:38.390
OK, it got something.

01:08:38.390 --> 01:08:43.250
But now you can compare it to
this, which is the optimum.

01:08:43.250 --> 01:08:45.439
This is the optimum
that you can get

01:08:45.439 --> 01:08:47.720
with one bit per source symbol.

01:08:47.720 --> 01:08:50.120
But you're going to
have to work hard.

01:08:50.120 --> 01:08:56.149
You're going to have to work
on a large block of data, not

01:08:56.149 --> 01:08:58.370
something as simple
as symbol by symbol,

01:08:58.370 --> 01:09:02.870
just one bit of quantization
for every component separately.

01:09:02.870 --> 01:09:06.920
And to get a sense for--

01:09:06.920 --> 01:09:09.140
maybe how hard you
might try to work

01:09:09.140 --> 01:09:12.890
and how much better
this might be,

01:09:12.890 --> 01:09:20.300
let's actually try to get
some intuition regarding

01:09:20.300 --> 01:09:22.399
the form of this thing.

01:09:22.399 --> 01:09:27.890
So if you look at the--

01:09:27.890 --> 01:09:30.470
if you look, typically--

01:09:30.470 --> 01:09:35.779
so with high
probability, so remember,

01:09:35.779 --> 01:09:40.370
we had this all these
discussions about typicality

01:09:40.370 --> 01:09:42.290
for discrete data.

01:09:42.290 --> 01:09:48.710
Well, you can also talk about
typicality of continuous data.

01:09:48.710 --> 01:09:52.760
And here, rather
than talk about sizes

01:09:52.760 --> 01:09:54.800
of sets in terms of
number of elements,

01:09:54.800 --> 01:09:56.960
you can talk about
volumes of sets.

01:09:56.960 --> 01:09:59.570
So here, with high
probability, if I

01:09:59.570 --> 01:10:08.030
look at realized n-tuple from
the source IID normal, 0, 1,

01:10:08.030 --> 01:10:12.830
then I can look at, let's
say, the sum of the square

01:10:12.830 --> 01:10:14.860
of the realized components.

01:10:18.950 --> 01:10:26.420
So these guys, the
square of these XI

01:10:26.420 --> 01:10:29.600
is what is its expectation.

01:10:32.210 --> 01:10:36.410
What is the expectation
of X squared?

01:10:42.470 --> 01:10:49.580
So X is normal 0, 0
mean variance one.

01:10:49.580 --> 01:10:52.938
What's the expectation of X
squared, second moment of X?

01:10:56.030 --> 01:10:56.530
1.

01:10:56.530 --> 01:10:57.100
Right.

01:10:57.100 --> 01:11:04.270
So here I'm looking at an
average of these guys, which

01:11:04.270 --> 01:11:09.540
are IID with expectation 1.

01:11:09.540 --> 01:11:12.330
So again, the law
of large numbers

01:11:12.330 --> 01:11:20.790
tells me that this average
is going to be around--

01:11:20.790 --> 01:11:22.350
with high probability,
this average

01:11:22.350 --> 01:11:27.540
is going to be close to
the expected value of each

01:11:27.540 --> 01:11:32.565
of these, which is one,
certainly no more than that.

01:11:35.840 --> 01:11:39.890
So this is, again, just kind
of a law of large numbers type

01:11:39.890 --> 01:11:42.080
argument.

01:11:42.080 --> 01:11:48.870
And if you'll allow
me to put the n here,

01:11:48.870 --> 01:11:51.180
and again to make
this rigorous, I

01:11:51.180 --> 01:11:54.660
need an epsilon and
all sorts of things.

01:11:54.660 --> 01:11:57.090
But I give you guys
credit that you

01:11:57.090 --> 01:12:01.068
don't need all the epsilons
to get the main point.

01:12:01.068 --> 01:12:02.610
So with high
probability, we're going

01:12:02.610 --> 01:12:08.610
to see a realized n-tuple, which
satisfies more or less this.

01:12:08.610 --> 01:12:13.530
Or, if you will, let's look at
the square root of this thing.

01:12:13.530 --> 01:12:24.330
OK, so if I think about the
realized n-tuple as a point

01:12:24.330 --> 01:12:29.580
in n-dimensional space, so let's
say that this board here now is

01:12:29.580 --> 01:12:36.450
all of Rn, then what I know
is that with all likelihood,

01:12:36.450 --> 01:12:43.590
the realized source n-tuple
is going to fall inside of--

01:12:43.590 --> 01:12:49.630
in n-dimensional ball of
radius essentially no more

01:12:49.630 --> 01:12:51.760
than square root of n.

01:12:58.200 --> 01:13:04.800
And now let's see what can
one possible reconstruction

01:13:04.800 --> 01:13:06.240
take care of.

01:13:06.240 --> 01:13:10.290
So in a sense, I
know that I need

01:13:10.290 --> 01:13:13.830
to worry about covering if I
want to achieve distortion D. I

01:13:13.830 --> 01:13:16.890
need to worry about covering--

01:13:16.890 --> 01:13:22.710
the sequences in this
typical ball, if you will.

01:13:22.710 --> 01:13:26.880
I need to cover all of
them within the distortion,

01:13:26.880 --> 01:13:29.550
essentially no more than D.

01:13:29.550 --> 01:13:34.350
Now, any particular possible
reconstruction code word,

01:13:34.350 --> 01:13:35.040
if you will--

01:13:38.030 --> 01:13:40.200
by the way, maybe
this is also a point

01:13:40.200 --> 01:13:49.380
to point out that talking about
a presentation of size m bits

01:13:49.380 --> 01:14:01.500
is actually equivalent to
having a codebook, a set

01:14:01.500 --> 01:14:10.520
of possible reconstructions
of size, let's call it

01:14:10.520 --> 01:14:18.140
capital M, which is
2 to the power m.

01:14:18.140 --> 01:14:28.540
So this is going to
be size of codebook,

01:14:28.540 --> 01:14:38.080
which is, in turn, the set
of possible reconstructions.

01:14:38.080 --> 01:14:45.790
Because you can think of any
one of these sequences of bits

01:14:45.790 --> 01:14:50.170
as if the decoder is going to
see a certain sequence of bits.

01:14:50.170 --> 01:14:53.710
It's going to come up with
one possible reconstruction.

01:14:53.710 --> 01:14:56.530
So if it has--

01:14:56.530 --> 01:15:01.930
you have 2 to the m
possible reconstructions

01:15:01.930 --> 01:15:04.780
that this decoder
might come up with.

01:15:04.780 --> 01:15:08.470
OK, so instead of talking
about the number of bits,

01:15:08.470 --> 01:15:12.280
I can talk about just
the number of codewords

01:15:12.280 --> 01:15:17.410
of possible reconstructions
that this decoder has.

01:15:17.410 --> 01:15:24.950
And so let's see, what one
particular reconstruction,

01:15:24.950 --> 01:15:27.470
how much how--

01:15:27.470 --> 01:15:33.170
what portion of the
possible source sequences

01:15:33.170 --> 01:15:36.950
is it going to be able to
cover to within distortion D.

01:15:36.950 --> 01:15:42.140
So basically, I'm asking
for a particular possible

01:15:42.140 --> 01:15:49.910
reconstruction, I'm asking, so
distortion D, again, per symbol

01:15:49.910 --> 01:15:52.490
distortion D would
correspond to--

01:15:56.030 --> 01:15:58.850
so it would correspond to--

01:15:58.850 --> 01:16:06.440
if we don't normalize,
it would correspond

01:16:06.440 --> 01:16:17.620
to the set of basically, a ball
of radius, no more than square

01:16:17.620 --> 01:16:19.960
root of nD.

01:16:19.960 --> 01:16:27.550
OK, so this so particular
possible reconstruction, this

01:16:27.550 --> 01:16:31.450
is the set the set
of sequences that

01:16:31.450 --> 01:16:34.630
are covered by
this reconstruction

01:16:34.630 --> 01:16:40.378
to within distortion,
no more than d

01:16:40.378 --> 01:16:44.500
is this ball of radius
square root of nD

01:16:44.500 --> 01:16:46.600
around the reconstruction point.

01:16:46.600 --> 01:16:51.435
OK, so Y square
root because if we

01:16:51.435 --> 01:16:52.810
if we don't take
the square root,

01:16:52.810 --> 01:16:57.890
then it's just the
sum of the squares.

01:16:57.890 --> 01:17:02.060
And that's how we define
the squared error,

01:17:02.060 --> 01:17:03.950
the sum of the squared.

01:17:03.950 --> 01:17:09.650
But with the square root, it
gives us the actual length

01:17:09.650 --> 01:17:11.840
of the vector.

01:17:11.840 --> 01:17:16.700
And then also we have the n
because we talk about distortion

01:17:16.700 --> 01:17:19.370
D. We normalize.

01:17:21.920 --> 01:17:25.490
Distortion D
corresponds to the sum

01:17:25.490 --> 01:17:28.520
of the square of the
components divided by n being

01:17:28.520 --> 01:17:34.910
no more than D. So the overall
sum is no more than nD,

01:17:34.910 --> 01:17:37.280
so the overall square
root of the sum

01:17:37.280 --> 01:17:38.960
is no more than
square root of nD.

01:17:42.830 --> 01:17:44.690
And so then the
question is, OK, well,

01:17:44.690 --> 01:17:57.500
if I want to get for
distortion no more than D,

01:17:57.500 --> 01:18:03.290
how many of these codewords
am I going to need in order

01:18:03.290 --> 01:18:05.000
to cover this typical set?

01:18:05.000 --> 01:18:15.140
Well, at least-- so size of
the codebook, this capital M,

01:18:15.140 --> 01:18:22.093
is going to have to be at least,
from a volume preservation

01:18:22.093 --> 01:18:23.510
argument, it's
going to have to be

01:18:23.510 --> 01:18:31.160
at least the ratio between
the volume of the big ball

01:18:31.160 --> 01:18:39.500
to the volume of these
smaller balls of radius D.

01:18:39.500 --> 01:18:44.090
OK, so the big ball is the
ball-- the typical set.

01:18:49.730 --> 01:18:57.370
It has a radius of square
root n, so it's some--

01:18:57.370 --> 01:19:01.040
n-dependent constant.

01:19:01.040 --> 01:19:03.170
But it's proportional
to the radius

01:19:03.170 --> 01:19:05.120
of the ball, which
is square root

01:19:05.120 --> 01:19:12.710
n to the power of
the dimension n.

01:19:12.710 --> 01:19:17.300
And then, the size
of this small ball

01:19:17.300 --> 01:19:21.500
is, again, the same
n-dependent constant.

01:19:21.500 --> 01:19:28.670
This time, the radius
is nD to the power n.

01:19:28.670 --> 01:19:30.110
OK, so that is--

01:19:37.710 --> 01:19:46.865
so that's 1/d to
the power n over 2.

01:19:50.520 --> 01:19:52.710
Sorry, I'm out of time.

01:19:52.710 --> 01:19:59.655
But hopefully, you guys can be
with me just to finish this.

01:20:04.430 --> 01:20:11.040
OK, so basically, that's
the size of the codebook.

01:20:11.040 --> 01:20:14.190
And I get this.

01:20:14.190 --> 01:20:18.765
OK, and so the rate that we're
going to need, equivalently

01:20:18.765 --> 01:20:21.540
to the rate, which is--

01:20:21.540 --> 01:20:22.290
what was the rate?

01:20:22.290 --> 01:20:23.160
I erased it.

01:20:23.160 --> 01:20:30.610
The number of bits divided by
the number of source symbols,

01:20:30.610 --> 01:20:34.930
which, in terms of the
size of the codebook--

01:20:34.930 --> 01:20:41.290
its log the size of the
codebook divided by n.

01:20:41.290 --> 01:20:46.630
That's going to have
to be at least--

01:20:46.630 --> 01:20:49.030
so size of the codebook
is at least this.

01:20:49.030 --> 01:20:58.870
So log of that is at least
n/2 log 1/D, and then

01:20:58.870 --> 01:21:07.020
divide it by N. So
that's the 1/2 log of 1/D

01:21:07.020 --> 01:21:11.790
that I just erased, the
rate-distortion function.

01:21:11.790 --> 01:21:16.500
This thing is 1/2, log of 1/D.

01:21:16.500 --> 01:21:21.030
So this volume
preservation argument

01:21:21.030 --> 01:21:25.650
tells you why you need
at least this much.

01:21:25.650 --> 01:21:34.950
You need-- the rate has to be
at least 1/2 log of 1 over D.

01:21:34.950 --> 01:21:40.260
And now, when you look at
this in two dimensions,

01:21:40.260 --> 01:21:43.590
you'd say, OK, but if I
want to actually cover

01:21:43.590 --> 01:21:46.950
this thing with
these smaller balls,

01:21:46.950 --> 01:21:49.395
I'm going to need a
substantial amount of overlap.

01:21:53.880 --> 01:21:58.410
But what happens in large
dimensions when n is large?

01:21:58.410 --> 01:22:00.480
In fact, you can be--

01:22:00.480 --> 01:22:06.240
this sort of covering
is very efficient.

01:22:06.240 --> 01:22:08.970
And the overlap here.

01:22:08.970 --> 01:22:10.710
So in two dimensions,
it's not true.

01:22:10.710 --> 01:22:13.410
But when n is large,
the amount of overlap

01:22:13.410 --> 01:22:16.920
becomes very insignificant.

01:22:16.920 --> 01:22:21.660
And so basically, you can
actually achieve this.

01:22:21.660 --> 01:22:24.360
You can actually--
you don't need

01:22:24.360 --> 01:22:31.440
more than essentially this ratio
of number of these small balls,

01:22:31.440 --> 01:22:34.140
so size of the codebook.

01:22:34.140 --> 01:22:37.890
And in fact, you can even--
and in fact, then you

01:22:37.890 --> 01:22:41.670
can ask, OK, well, how do I
actually choose these codewords,

01:22:41.670 --> 01:22:42.990
these ball centers?

01:22:42.990 --> 01:22:44.130
How do I choose them?

01:22:44.130 --> 01:22:48.030
You don't even have to be
too careful, too judicious.

01:22:48.030 --> 01:22:52.720
You can actually randomly
select these points.

01:22:55.492 --> 01:22:57.700
You can characterize
the distribution

01:22:57.700 --> 01:23:02.920
from which you merely need
to sample randomly to get

01:23:02.920 --> 01:23:04.330
these reconstruction points.

01:23:04.330 --> 01:23:07.030
And that's, in fact,
the way that we

01:23:07.030 --> 01:23:13.720
prove that this minimum mutual
information is achievable.

01:23:13.720 --> 01:23:18.520
It's true specifically for this
example in the Gaussian case,

01:23:18.520 --> 01:23:25.480
but in fact, quite generally,
this sort of picture

01:23:25.480 --> 01:23:30.310
carries over for general
sources, general distributions,

01:23:30.310 --> 01:23:33.790
distortion criteria, and so on.

01:23:33.790 --> 01:23:37.390
I know this was
a lot to take in.

01:23:37.390 --> 01:23:42.800
And I also apologize for
taking more of your time

01:23:42.800 --> 01:23:45.860
than I deserved.

01:23:45.860 --> 01:23:48.890
So I guess now
you have no time--

01:23:48.890 --> 01:23:51.250
you have minutes four to--

01:23:51.250 --> 01:23:51.750
It's OK.

01:23:51.750 --> 01:23:53.040
[LAUGHS]

01:23:53.040 --> 01:23:55.910
[INAUDIBLE] of slides, which
have last year's solution.

01:23:55.910 --> 01:23:58.730
And we looked at the
plot in the last class,

01:23:58.730 --> 01:24:01.190
where we looked at
how we [INAUDIBLE]

01:24:01.190 --> 01:24:03.830
point, which Saki was
making, that in practice, we

01:24:03.830 --> 01:24:06.680
can get some
distortion performance,

01:24:06.680 --> 01:24:09.710
and how does that compare to
the optimal performance, which

01:24:09.710 --> 01:24:14.390
